On a stormy winter night, the lighthouse at Point Hollow flickered weakly, its beam struggling against the howling wind. Elias Grant, the lighthouse keeper, had been alone for years, his only company the sea and the occasional seabird. But tonight, something was different.

As he adjusted the lamp, a faint crackle filled the air. Elias froze. He had never heard anything like it. The signal was weak, almost like a whisper, and it came from a direction no ship had ever been known to travel.

Curious, he turned on the old radio, its dial creaking as he adjusted it. The signal grew stronger, and then—words. Not in any language he recognized, but they carried a strange rhythm, almost musical. It was as if the sea itself was speaking.

Elias sat in his creaking chair, heart pounding. He tried to call back, but the signal was gone. He tried again, and again, but the static returned, and with it, a new message: *“Come to the shore.”*

